title: math_context
---
// Math Town dialogue. Keep node titles aligned with the town placements.
// Canter's freight-manifest choice must set orbital_manifest_signed.
// This node has no placement.
===

title: erdish
---
Erdish: My mind is open.
-> The cafe has a door.
    Erdish: I saw it. The window was nearer.
    Erdish: Emmy says I have to use the door when I leave. She's drawn a diagram.
-> What are you doing at this table?
    Erdish: Emmy let me have one sheet of paper. I needed somewhere to put the other eleven.
    Erdish: She has taken away my chalk.
-> Are you always like this?
    Erdish: You sound like the man whose attic I visited.
    Erdish: He had an excellent collection of old maps. Very poor hospitality.
-> Goodbye.
===

title: trent
---
Trent: Haven't seen you on Euclid Street. I'm Trent. Do you want an introduction, or are you managing?
-> Who should I meet?
    Trent: Emmy if you're hungry. Walter if you have paperwork. Peggy if you like an argument you can't win by shouting.
    Trent: I'd visit Emmy first. Walter has a form for visitors and he's running short of ink.
-> Why do you introduce strangers?
    Trent: My first day here, I asked for the observatory and got directions to the sewer.
    Trent: Three people waved as I climbed down. I know all three now.
-> Can I trust you?
    Trent: You can ask Emmy what I owe her. She'll tell you the number down to the penny.
    Trent: Then you decide.
-> Do you know Erdish?
    Trent: Everyone knows Erdish. That's half the problem.
    Trent: If he says he's been invited, ask by whom.
-> I'll find my own way.
    Trent: Fair enough. Watch the stair above the gate. The last step is taller than it looks.
===

title: walter
---
Walter: Stop by the ledger, please. Every arrival gets a line. I have an entry here that says "came through the window."
-> That was Erdish.
    Walter: Of course it was. I made a separate column for him and he has already filled it.
-> What are you guarding against?
    Walter: An unsigned message came through with the freight yesterday. "Weather is fine." No sender. No destination.
    Walter: I sent it back. This morning I got another one: "Weather is still fine."
-> Could it be an ordinary letter?
    Walter: Certainly. I would love an ordinary letter. I checked the ink, the seals, even the fold.
    Walter: It's probably somebody laughing at me. That possibility has not improved my mood.
-> Is the gate safe?
    Walter: The ring's engineers sign off on the machinery. I sign off on who came through it.
    Walter: As far as I can tell, you arrived with your own head attached. Put your name here.
-> I'll leave you to the ledger.
    Walter: Good. There are two pages I haven't mistrusted yet.
===

title: busy_beaver
---
Busy Beaver: Careful by that scaffold. I told the gate crew I'd finish the cradle before lunch.
-> Is it almost finished?
    Busy Beaver: That depends on which lunch you mean.
    Busy Beaver: The foreman came back with a clock. I told him a clock doesn't tell you when a machine will stop.
-> What's holding you up?
    Busy Beaver: Every time I test one arrangement, I find another arrangement that runs longer.
    Busy Beaver: Come back tomorrow. I may have an answer, or a taller scaffold.
-> Could you just stop testing?
    Busy Beaver: I could. Would you trust a gate cradle I stopped testing because I was hungry?
    Busy Beaver: ...Don't answer that. I am hungry.
-> Why do they call you Busy Beaver?
    Busy Beaver: They tried "Project Completion Estimate" for a while. It made everyone cross.
    Busy Beaver: Beaver will do.
-> I'll stay clear.
    Busy Beaver: Good. If anyone asks, I was on my break when you arrived.
===

title: peggy
---
Peggy: Victor has set me another test. He wants the way through the terraces. I intend to keep it to myself.
-> How can he tell you know it?
    Peggy: I go behind the wall where he can't see me. Then he chooses which end I should come back from.
    Peggy: I can do either. Someone guessing the way would have trouble doing that twenty-seven times.
-> Couldn't you get lucky?
    Peggy: For one round, sure. He knows that. It's why I've been at this since breakfast.
    Peggy: You should see him count on his fingers when he thinks I'm looking away.
-> Why keep the route secret?
    Peggy: Erdish is asking everyone about it. Last month he appeared in someone's pantry to ask where the stairs went.
    Peggy: I don't want him learning the pantry route.
-> Do you ever tell Victor anything true?
    Peggy: Every answer. He's free to check them. He just doesn't get the map.
    Peggy: He gets annoyed when I say that. Go ask him.
-> Good luck with your test.
    Peggy: Thanks. Tell Victor I said that too. He won't believe you.
===

title: victor
---
Victor: Twenty-seven correct answers. I asked Peggy to return from the east passage seventeen times. She did. Then the west.
-> Are you convinced?
    Victor: That she knows a route through? Yes. I'd wager on it.
    Victor: That I know the route now? Not a chance. My notes are a list of my own questions.
-> Why so many tests?
    Victor: A guess can work once. I thought I could catch her guessing.
    Victor: After the twelfth round she offered me a chair. After the twentieth she brought me tea.
-> Couldn't she be tricking you?
    Victor: She could have an accomplice. Or a tunnel I haven't found. I wrote both down.
    Victor: She calls that being thorough. Usually with her eyes closed.
-> Do you two get along?
    Victor: We've argued for years. This is the first argument with a scorecard.
-> I'll let you concentrate.
    Victor: If Peggy asks, I'm not tired. I'm checking the west passage again.
===

title: data_lovelace
---
Data Lovelace: Don't lean on the card stack, please. If I drop it, I'll spend the evening sorting the holes.
-> What does the engine do?
    Data Lovelace: It takes the cards, follows the operations, and gives me columns of numbers.
    Data Lovelace: Right now I'm trying to make a pattern of flowers for the loom. The petals keep coming out square.
-> Why use cards?
    Data Lovelace: The loom already understands them. I showed the machinist my arithmetic and he fetched a box of needles.
    Data Lovelace: I'd prefer he had fetched a better engine, but we work with what we have.
-> Can it make something you didn't expect?
    Data Lovelace: It has. Yesterday I used the wrong card and got a lovely pattern of little windows.
    Data Lovelace: I kept the pattern. I corrected the card.
-> Do the others help?
    Data Lovelace: Emmy checks my calculations. Busy Beaver says he'll build me a faster motor.
    Data Lovelace: He's been saying that for a year. I rather like the old motor now.
-> I'll leave your cards alone.
    Data Lovelace: Thank you. The pile on the left is in order. The pile on the right is having a difficult day.
===

title: olivia
---
Olivia: One question at a time, please. People come back angry when they ask two things and hear only the first answer.
-> Can the gate take me home?
    Olivia: If you know the address. I've never seen your home, so you'll have to be more precise than that.
    Olivia: Walter keeps the destination register by the gate.
-> What should I ask you?
    Olivia: You've just asked me. Shall we try again?
-> Do you know what happens tomorrow?
    Olivia: I know the observatory is opening at dawn. Hypatia booked the telescope.
    Olivia: I know less about whether the clouds will cooperate.
-> Why do people call you an oracle?
    Olivia: Someone asked me whether a locked door could open. I said yes. He spent a week waiting beside it.
    Olivia: His wife eventually fetched a key.
-> I think that's all.
    Olivia: Good. I can put the tea back on.
===

title: leib_knives
---
Gottfried Leib-Knives: Watch your sleeve. This blade is sharpened, and you nearly walked straight into it.
-> Are you practicing on the street?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: The fencing hall is shut. Someone dropped a proof on the floor and nobody will step across it.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: I asked them to move the paper. They asked me to refute it first.
-> What's with all the symbols?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: I'm measuring how quickly a distance changes. If my opponent closes in twice as fast, I get half as long to react.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: You can test the arithmetic with your feet. Keep your hands away from the point.
-> Can you show me a move?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Take one step back. There. A longer reach buys a little time.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Don't copy my stance. You have different knees.
-> Does anyone beat you?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Peggy did. She insisted I choose my opening before she chose where to stand.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Victor said her victory was statistically significant. I dislike them both this week.
-> I'll give you room.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Sensible. That corner's safer.
===

title: hypatia_prime
---
Hypatia: A little to the left, please. The telescope mounting is loose, and I don't trust anyone leaning on it.
-> What are you watching?
    Hypatia: A point of light beside the orbital station. Last night it passed behind the moon.
    Hypatia: Davy called it a speck on the lens. I've cleaned the lens six times.
-> Did you build this telescope?
    Hypatia: I rebuilt most of it. The drawings survived in three different copies, each missing a different page.
    Hypatia: The lens was the easy part. Somebody had used the brass rings for curtain weights.
-> Aren't you a reconstruction?
    Hypatia: Yes. The first thing I asked for was a proper workspace.
    Hypatia: People keep asking whether I remember things that nobody wrote down. I wish I did.
-> Why study the old diagrams?
    Hypatia: Two copies put the same star in different places. One of the scribes got the date wrong.
    Hypatia: If I can find the original measurements, I'll know which one.
-> I'll stay out of the way.
    Hypatia: You can stay. Just don't touch that screw. It's the only one that fits.
===

title: emmy_noether_cafe
---
Emmy Ethereal: Welcome to the Convergent Series. The table near the window is free. Erdish has occupied three others with paper.
-> What do you recommend?
    Emmy Ethereal: Tea. The coffee is coming once I find out which of Erdish's cups is mine.
    Emmy Ethereal: He started writing on the saucers. I have stopped lending him saucers.
-> What's everyone arguing about?
    Emmy Ethereal: Canter wants to count the biscuits. Davy says I should always make room for one more customer.
    Emmy Ethereal: I have twelve biscuits. Both of them are welcome to look at the tray.
-> Why is there a theorem on the menu?
    Emmy Ethereal: Someone asked what I was working on. I wrote a line on the board. It attracted six mathematicians and nobody ordered lunch.
    Emmy Ethereal: If the laws stay the same as time passes, energy is conserved. In the calculation, at least.
-> Is Erdish causing trouble?
    Emmy Ethereal: He came through the window again. He can reach the door from where he's sitting.
    Emmy Ethereal: I've drawn him a map. He says he's improving it.
-> I'll find a seat.
    Emmy Ethereal: Thank you. Tell me if the table wobbles. That much I can fix.
===

title: georg_canter
---
<<if condition("world.flag_set", "orbital_manifest_signed")>>
    Georg Canter: Twelve lenses. I counted them myself. Customs has my signature and, I hope, no further questions.
    -> Why did you write aleph-null?
        Georg Canter: The form had a box large enough for a joke. I regret underestimating the customs officer.
        Georg Canter: I sent an apology. She returned it for lacking a reference number.
    -> Do you really study infinity?
        Georg Canter: Yes. If you have an afternoon, bring me a list of all the infinite strings of zeros and ones.
        Georg Canter: I'll show you one you missed.
    -> I'll get back to the station.
<<elseif condition("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_query")>>
    Georg Canter: Ah. Customs is asking about crate 0447. I wondered how long they'd let that manifest sit.
    -> Please sign a finite count.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "orbital_manifest_signed" true>>
        Georg Canter: Twelve survey lenses. No extras under the packing foam. Here, signed.
        Georg Canter: Tell Officer Okafor I'm sorry. She deserved a number she could use.
    -> Why did the manifest say aleph-null?
        Georg Canter: There were twelve lenses and a great deal of foam. I was making a joke about counting.
        Georg Canter: The gate clerk did not laugh. I can hardly blame her.
    -> What are all those lists for?
        Georg Canter: People insisted that every number could be put on a numbered list. I've been arguing with them for years.
        Georg Canter: Do you want to see the page where the argument failed?
    -> I'll return later.
<<else>>
    Georg Canter: Please mind the stacks. The office lent me one cabinet. I'm afraid I'm using four.
    -> What do you count here?
        Georg Canter: Survey lenses, building plots, the steps to the gate. Things that will fit in a ledger.
        Georg Canter: On the other desk I've been trying to list numbers that refuse to fit.
    -> Can't you list every number?
        Georg Canter: Start with every possible infinite string of zeros and ones. Put them in a numbered list.
        Georg Canter: I can change the first bit of the first entry, the second of the second, and so on. You missed that new string.
    -> Is that a freight manifest?
        Georg Canter: Yes. The orbital station ordered twelve lenses. I've sent them a joke in the quantity box.
        Georg Canter: I expect to hear from customs.
    -> I'll leave you to it.
<<endif>>
===

title: davy_hylbert
---
Davy Hylbert: Our observatory guest book is full. I can still fit you in. The caretaker is going to object.
-> How, if every room is taken?
    Davy Hylbert: In a hotel with rooms numbered forever, move guest one to room two, guest two to room three, and so on.
    Davy Hylbert: Room one is free. I have the plan on the back of a receipt. The caretaker wants actual keys.
-> What if a whole crowd arrives?
    Davy Hylbert: An endless crowd? Move each old guest from room n to room twice n. The odd-numbered rooms open up.
    Davy Hylbert: It works beautifully on paper. Last time I tried it here, people refused to leave their beds.
-> Does the observatory really have infinite rooms?
    Davy Hylbert: Twelve. And one cot behind the telescope.
    Davy Hylbert: I can offer you the cot, though Hypatia will be working until dawn.
-> What is Hypatia looking for?
    Davy Hylbert: Something moving past the moon. I told her it was dirt on the glass.
    Davy Hylbert: She's cleaned it six times. I'd better go and have another look.
-> I'll come back another night.
    Davy Hylbert: Sensible. I might have settled the room numbers by then.
===
